(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 19-year-old woman whose sister developed a large keloid after ear piercing asks how to avoid the same problem. What is described as the most important element in the management of keloids?","choices":["Compression therapy applied for one week after any injury","Prevention, including advising high-risk patients to avoid cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing","Routine biopsy of every lesion to exclude malignancy","Early surgical excision of every lesion as soon as it appears, followed by observation alone afterwards","Intralesional steroid injection as the sole modality"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prevention is described as the most important treatment. High-risk patients should be advised to avoid cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing, and post-surgical education includes avoiding stretching of an immature scar and avoiding hot baths. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has a raised, firm scar that developed within four weeks of a surgical incision. It remains within the original incision line and has flattened slightly over the past year. What management options apply?","choices":["Systemic corticosteroids tapered over six weeks","No treatment is ever appropriate because hypertrophic scars always resolve completely and spontaneously without any intervention over time","Intralesional corticosteroid or 5-fluorouracil, compression therapy and silicone sheeting, surgical excision, or pulsed dye laser","Excision alone, which is curative without adjunct because recurrence does not occur","Cryotherapy is contraindicated in all scar management"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypertrophic scar management includes intralesional injection of corticosteroid or 5-fluorouracil, compression therapy and silicone sheeting, surgical excision, and pulsed dye laser, which reduces scar erythema by reducing neovascularisation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has a single asymptomatic annular ring of flesh-coloured papules on the dorsum of the foot. What proportion of cases does this variant represent, and what is the expected course?","choices":["About 50 percent of cases, and the condition invariably recurs after clearing","About 75 percent of cases, and lifelong systemic immunosuppression is required","About 75 percent of cases, and the condition progresses to malignancy without treatment","About 75 percent of cases, and the condition is benign and self-limiting","About 25 percent of cases, and the condition is benign and self-limiting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The localised variant accounts for about 75 percent of cases, presenting as flesh-coloured to erythematous papules in an annular ring on the dorsal hands, feet, and ankles, and is asymptomatic. Granuloma annulare is a benign, self-limiting granulomatous dermatosis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has scaly patches on the scalp with areas of hair loss and visible black dots where hairs have fractured. A potassium hydroxide preparation confirms fungal elements. His mother asks whether a medicated cream will be enough. What is the correct response?","choices":["Oral therapy is required, because topical agents alone do not penetrate the infected hair shaft","No treatment is needed because tinea capitis resolves spontaneously after puberty","A topical corticosteroid should be used first to settle the inflammation","An antifungal shampoo used two to three times weekly is sufficient on its own","A topical antifungal cream applied to the lesion and 1 to 2 cm beyond will clear it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinea capitis requires oral therapy; topical agents alone do not penetrate the infected hair shaft. Antifungal shampoo reduces viable spore shedding but does not replace oral treatment. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man presents with a rash that began on the backs of his hands and spread toward the trunk over two days. Examination shows numerous lesions with three concentric zones — a dusky centre, a pale ring, and an erythematous outer rim — on the dorsal hands, forearms, and knees. Individual lesions have remained fixed in place for four days. He had a cold sore on his lip ten days ago. Less than 5 percent of his body surface is involved and there are no mucosal erosions. What is the most likely trigger?","choices":["No identifiable trigger","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Herpes simplex virus","Epstein-Barr virus","A sulfonamide antibiotic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Over 50 percent of erythema multiforme cases are triggered by herpes simplex virus, making it the most common precipitant, and a history of recurrent infection is a listed risk factor. The cold sole ten days before the eruption supplies exactly that history. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 42-year-old Black woman has multiple small, smooth, firm dark brown papules 1 to 3 mm on the face and neck. What is the diagnosis, and how does it relate to seborrheic keratosis?","choices":["Melanocytic naevi, which peak in number in the third decade","Multiple lentigines, which occur on sun-exposed skin only","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans, which is a premalignant variant of seborrheic keratosis","Syringomas, which are eccrine duct neoplasms","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans, whose lesions are identical to small seborrheic keratoses"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Dermatosis papulosa nigrans consists of multiple small, smooth, firm black or dark brown papules 1 to 5 mm on the face and neck, identical to small seborrheic keratoses. It is thought to be genetic and is believed to be a developmental defect of the hair follicle. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with thickened, discoloured toenails is having a specimen taken to confirm onychomycosis before oral therapy is started. From where should the specimen be taken?","choices":["Any nail on the affected foot, whether or not it appears diseased","A plucked hair from the affected limb","The most proximal accessible diseased nail bed or subungual debris","The surrounding proximal nail fold","The distal free edge of the nail plate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sampling should be from the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed and subungual debris, where viable organism is concentrated, using potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid-Schiff stain of clippings, fungal culture, or polymerase chain reaction. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman is given a topical antifungal for a single annular scaly plaque on the trunk. How far should the medication be applied?","choices":["To the visible lesion only, stopping as soon as it clears","Application distance does not matter provided oral antifungal therapy is also given alongside the topical cream","To the centre of the lesion only, where the infection began","To the whole limb, to prevent spread","To the lesion and 1 to 2 cm beyond its border, continuing through the recommended course after visible improvement"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Topical terbinafine, butenafine, or an azole is applied to the lesion and 1 to 2 cm beyond its border, using the product-specific frequency and duration and continuing through the recommended course after visible improvement. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man bitten by a spider with a red hourglass on the underside of its abdomen develops localised erythema, piloerection, and sweating around the bite within 30 minutes, followed by severe crampy abdominal pain and muscle spasms. What treatments are used for envenomation?","choices":["Doxycycline for 5 to 10 days","Surgical debridement of the necrotic bite site with delayed reconstruction of the resulting defect","Topical permethrin applied overnight","Calcium gluconate 10 percent, narcotic analgesics, muscle relaxants, and antivenom in selected cases","Albendazole and ivermectin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Latrodectus mactans, the southern black widow, has a characteristic red hourglass. Envenomation treatment includes calcium gluconate 10 percent, narcotic analgesics, and muscle relaxants, with hospitalisation depending on symptoms and increased risk in the very old, very young, and those with cardiovascular disease. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with sudden onset of fever to 39.1 degrees Celsius, chills, and myalgias, followed within a day by a painful red area on her left shin. Examination shows a brightly erythematous plaque raised above the surrounding skin with a sharply defined border, and red streaks extending proximally. She has tinea pedis. What is the most likely organism and the drug of choice?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus, treated with cephalexin","Corynebacterium minutissimum, treated with topical erythromycin","Streptococcus pyogenes, treated with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, treated with ciprofloxacin","Streptococcus pyogenes, treated with penicillin V"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Group A streptococcus is the most common organism in erysipelas, which involves the upper dermis and superficial cutaneous lymphatics. Penicillin V is the pharmacotherapy, with clindamycin if the patient is penicillin allergic. Tinea pedis is a listed risk factor. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with recurrent tinea pedis is being counselled on preventing further episodes. What patient education point is described as essential?","choices":["Walking barefoot in community showers to allow air drying","Drying between the toes after bathing","Wearing occlusive footwear to protect the skin","Sharing antifungal foot powder with household members","Soaking the feet daily in warm water"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Drying between the toes after bathing is marked as essential, alongside antifungal foot powder for shoes, wearing open-toed sandals when possible, using sandals in community showers, and changing socks frequently. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 19-year-old returns from a tropical beach holiday with an intensely itchy, raised, serpentine track on the foot that has advanced a few centimetres over the past two days. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","choices":["Cutaneous larva migrans, treated with surgical excision or cryotherapy","Cutaneous larva migrans, treated with topical therapy alone","Scabies, treated with whole-body permethrin","Cutaneous larva migrans, treated with oral albendazole or ivermectin","Tungiasis, treated with excision of the embedded flea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous larva migrans results from larvae of animal nematodes, mostly dog and cat hookworms, in which the human is a dead-end host. The classic lesion is an erythematous, raised, vesicular, linear or serpentine trail progressing 2 to 3 cm per day. Treatment is albendazole 400 mg daily for 3 days or ivermectin. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy presents with widespread superficial skin peeling and a positive Nikolsky sign but entirely normal mucous membranes. What diagnosis does the preserved mucosa most suggest?","choices":["Erythema multiforme major","Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms","Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome spares the mucosa and is toxin-mediated, which is exactly how it is separated from Stevens-Johnson syndrome on the differential. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with atopic dermatitis affecting both the eyelids and the extensor forearms asks how to use the two creams she has been prescribed. What guidance about potency selection is correct?","choices":["Use a low-potency or non-steroidal agent on the face and eyelids, and a low to medium potency agent on the body","Avoid topical corticosteroids at both sites and use emollients alone","Use a high-potency corticosteroid on the face and eyelids, and a low to medium potency agent on the body","Use clobetasol propionate 0.05 percent at both sites for two weeks","Use the same high-potency agent at both sites for consistency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Potency is selected according to site and severity: low-potency or non-steroid agents for the face, and low to medium potency for the body. Calcineurin inhibitors such as tacrolimus and pimecrolimus are options for sensitive areas such as the face and eyelids. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a newly diagnosed melanoma asks whether his lymph nodes will be sampled. At what thresholds is sentinel lymph node biopsy offered or discussed in melanoma?","choices":["Only at a thickness of 4 mm or greater","Based on lesion diameter rather than Breslow thickness","Only when clinically palpable nodes are present","At any Breslow thickness, since every melanoma warrants sentinel lymph node biopsy regardless of ulceration, mitotic rate, or other histologic risk factors","At a Breslow thickness of 1.0 mm or greater, or 0.8 mm or greater with additional histologic risk factors such as ulceration or a high mitotic rate"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Sentinel lymph node biopsy is offered or discussed at 1.0 mm Breslow thickness or greater, or at 0.8 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors such as ulceration or a high mitotic rate are present. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman is examined and a flat, non-elevated, non-depressed pigmented lesion measuring 1.4 cm is documented on the forearm. What term correctly describes this lesion?","choices":["Plaque","Papule","Patch","Macule","Nodule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A patch is a flat lesion greater than 1 cm without elevation or depression. At 1.4 cm this lesion crosses the size threshold that separates a patch from a macule. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has an annular eruption of the forearms and the clinician is distinguishing erythema multiforme from urticaria. What feature most reliably separates them?","choices":["Urticarial wheals remain fixed in place for several days, whereas erythema multiforme lesions migrate and resolve within hours of appearing","Only urticaria involves the extremities","Only erythema multiforme blanches with pressure","Urticarial wheals are migratory and individual lesions last less than 24 hours, whereas erythema multiforme produces fixed target lesions","Only erythema multiforme is pruritic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The differential states that urticarial wheals are pruritic, migratory, and blanch fully, with no fixed target lesions and individual lesions lasting less than 24 hours. Fixity over days is what identifies erythema multiforme. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 24-year-old traveller returns from a rural area having walked barefoot on sandy soil. He has several erythematous papules on the feet that have enlarged over weeks into firm, somewhat translucent yellow nodules 4 to 10 mm across, some painful. What is the diagnosis and how is it confirmed?","choices":["Cercarial dermatitis, confirmed by history of freshwater exposure","Scabies, confirmed by skin scraping","Tungiasis, confirmed by dermoscopy visualising ovoid eggs","Cutaneous larva migrans, confirmed by the serpentine trail","Bedbug bites, confirmed by inspection of the sleeping area"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tungiasis is infestation by penetration of the adult female flea into human skin to lay eggs. Solitary or multiple erythematous papules enlarge over weeks to 4 to 10 mm, sometimes forming a firm translucent nodule, and dermoscopy is used to visualise the ovoid eggs. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 16-year-old football player develops acne confined to the shoulders and upper back under his pads. What predisposing factor does this represent?","choices":["Endocrine acne from insulin resistance","Emotional stress causing cortisol release","Acne mechanica, from pressure occluding the pilosebaceous follicle","Drug-induced acne from an anabolic steroid","Genetic predisposition, given the three-fold risk with an affected first-degree relative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acne mechanica results from pressure on the skin — shoulder pads, orthopaedic casts, and helmets are the named examples — occluding the pilosebaceous follicle. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has an intensely pruritic eruption of violaceous flat-topped papules on both wrists, and a biopsy is taken from one lesion. What histopathological finding is most characteristic?","choices":["Subepithelial blistering with neutrophils at the dermal-epidermal junction","Hyperkeratosis with prominent parakeratosis and neutrophilic microabscesses","Granulomatous inflammation surrounding degenerated collagen","A band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis","Acantholysis with intraepithelial blistering"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy in lichen planus shows band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis. Additional characteristic findings include hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis, vacuolisation of the basal layer, Civatte bodies in the lower epidermis, and saw-tooth shaped rete ridges. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 177"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with a rapidly expanding painful ulcer of the shin is being investigated for an underlying systemic cause. What systemic conditions are most closely associated with pyoderma gangrenosum?","choices":["Inflammatory bowel disease, inflammatory arthritis, and haematologic disorders","Sarcoidosis and streptococcal infection","Type 2 diabetes, polycystic ovary syndrome, and gastrointestinal malignancy","Celiac disease and autoimmune thyroid disease","Chronic venous insufficiency and peripheral arterial disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pyoderma gangrenosum is a neutrophilic dermatosis with dysregulated innate immune activation, and inflammatory bowel disease heads the list of key associated conditions alongside inflammatory arthritis and haematologic disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with alcohol use disorder presents with severe leg pain following a minor injury. Over hours the skin changes from red-purple to blue-grey, bullae with thick purple fluid appear, and the area that was exquisitely tender becomes numb. He is febrile and tachycardic. What is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Start broad-spectrum antibiotics and reassess in 24 hours","Perform bedside incision and drainage in the emergency department","Immediate surgical consultation for aggressive debridement, without waiting for imaging or laboratory results","Admit for observation and elevate the limb","Obtain magnetic resonance imaging to localise the site and depth of infection before consulting surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency with high mortality, and laboratory tests and imaging studies should not delay surgical intervention. Loss of tenderness reflects destruction of superficial nerves and is an ominous sign. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl has small light brown macules on the nose that fade each winter, while her 68-year-old grandmother has darker macules on the hands and trunk that do not change with the seasons. How does a simple lentigo differ from an ephelis in its relationship to sun exposure?","choices":["Lentigines occur only on sun-exposed skin and fade during the winter months, whereas ephelides occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas and persist year round","Lentigines occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas and do not fade in winter, whereas ephelides are most prominent on sun-exposed skin and fade seasonally","Lentigines require excision in every case","Lentigines are always larger than 2 cm","Lentigines never occur on mucous membranes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lentigines are benign, well-circumscribed, round to oval uniformly black or brown macules under 5 mm occurring on skin, conjunctiva, and mucocutaneous surfaces, on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas. Ephelides are most pronounced in spring and summer and fade in winter. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A clinician searches for published evidence to answer a specific question about a 65-year-old smoker with chest pain and haemoptysis, and finds none that applies. What are the limitations of applying evidence-based medicine at the bedside?","choices":["It is time consuming and many clinical questions do not have relevant evidence available","It cannot be combined with clinical guidelines","It removes the need for clinical judgement","It always produces a definitive answer to the clinical question but is prohibitively expensive to apply","It applies only to surgical decisions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The limitations given are that the process is time consuming and that many clinical questions do not have relevant evidence to answer them. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 7-year-old has a solitary, asymptomatic, pink, hairless, firm dome-shaped papule on the cheek that grew over several weeks and has since been stable. What is the diagnosis, and what is the diagnostic concern?","choices":["Common acquired melanocytic nevus, which is uniformly brown","Pyogenic granuloma, which bleeds easily after trauma","Pigmented spindle cell nevus, which is darkly pigmented","Spitz nevus, which is usually benign but sometimes resembles melanoma","Molluscum contagiosum, with central umbilication"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A Spitz nevus is usually benign with a phase of growth, fast or slow, followed by a stable period. It is solitary, asymptomatic, pink or red, hairless, firm, and dome-shaped, several millimetres to centimetres, usually on the face, neck, or extremities, and it sometimes resembles melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with hidradenitis suppurativa who smokes and is obese asks what she can do herself to reduce flares. Which measure is described as essential?","choices":["Shaving the affected areas daily with a multi-blade razor","Daily vigorous scrubbing of the affected areas","Applying heat to the axillae daily","Smoking cessation","Wearing tight-fitting garments to support the affected areas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is described as essential among the preventive measures, alongside avoiding heat exposure, daily cleansing with an antibacterial soap or chlorhexidine or benzoyl peroxide wash, weight loss if obese, and avoiding constrictive clothing and frictional trauma. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman living alone reports generalised itching and rough, flaking skin each winter, worse on the lower legs. She takes long hot showers daily using a scented bar soap. Examination shows dry skin with fine scale and a few superficial fissures on the shins, without erythematous plaques or vesicles. What is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Begin a high-potency topical corticosteroid to the legs twice daily","Apply a keratolytic containing urea or lactic acid directly to the fissures","Take short lukewarm showers and apply a thick ointment or cream within minutes of bathing","Switch to a scented moisturising body wash used generously over the whole body daily","Continue daily hot showers but apply a moisturiser several hours afterwards"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Xerosis reflects impaired stratum corneum hydration promoted by aging, low humidity, hot water, and detergents. Management is short lukewarm showers, a gentle fragrance-free cleanser used only where needed, and a thick ointment or cream applied within minutes of bathing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 153"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman develops an itchy eruption of 2 to 5 mm erythematous papules on the décolletage and forearms a few hours after her first sunny weekend of the spring. The face, which is exposed year-round, is unaffected. What additional test is considered mandatory?","choices":["Skin biopsy in every patient before treatment","A potassium hydroxide preparation of the affected skin","Serum tryptase to exclude mastocytosis","Photopatch testing to identify a photoallergen","An antinuclear antibody panel to exclude lupus"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. An antinuclear antibody panel is mandatory to exclude lupus, particularly anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies. Polymorphous light eruption is otherwise largely a clinical diagnosis based on history and morphology. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several soft yellowish papules with a central dell on the forehead and is worried they are skin cancer. What is the pathophysiology of sebaceous hyperplasia, and what is its malignant potential?","choices":["Ultraviolet-induced keratinocytic dysplasia in a field of sun-damaged skin that may progress to squamous cell carcinoma","Overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue, with progressive local invasion","Reduced turnover of sebocytes with age causing gland enlargement, with no known potential for malignant transformation","Capillary proliferation, with a risk of haemorrhage","Proliferation of eccrine ducts, with a low risk of malignant transformation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sebaceous hyperplasia is a common benign condition in which the turnover of sebocytes, the sebum-producing epithelial cells, changes with age. It has no known potential for malignant transformation, though patients often present with malignancy concern. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 3-week-old infant, born prematurely, has a bright red papule on the scalp that was a faint blanched area with fine telangiectasias at birth and has grown noticeably in the past fortnight. What growth pattern should the parents be told to expect?","choices":["A rapid proliferative phase with most growth in the first 4 to 6 months, followed by a slower involution phase","Immediate involution beginning at birth with complete resolution by about 6 weeks of age without a proliferative phase","No change in size at any point during childhood","Rapid growth with a high risk of malignant transformation","Steady growth in proportion to the child with no involution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Infantile hemangiomas have a unique growth pattern of rapid proliferation followed by slower involution, with rapid growth during the neonatal period and most growth in the first 4 to 6 months. The earliest sign is blanching of the involved skin followed by fine telangiectasias and then a red macule. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has a soft, mobile, non-tender subcutaneous mass on the upper back that has been slowly enlarging for years. What is the most likely diagnosis and appropriate management?","choices":["Lipoma, the most common soft tissue tumour, which can be observed if asymptomatic","Epidermoid cyst, which requires surgical excision of the entire capsule when not inflamed","Liposarcoma, requiring urgent wide excision","Dermatofibroma, which shows a positive dimple sign","Abscess, requiring incision and drainage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A lipoma is a benign localised overgrowth of fat cells in subcutaneous tissue and the most common soft tissue tumour. Diagnosis is typically clinical, and asymptomatic tumours can be observed, with excision considered for cosmetically deforming or enlarging lesions. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has a suspicious pigmented growth on the back that the clinician wishes to sample in a way that removes the entire lesion with deep and wide margins. What biopsy technique is appropriate?","choices":["Shave biopsy","Excisional biopsy","Fine needle aspiration of the lesion","Curettage of the lesion surface","Punch biopsy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An excisional biopsy removes the whole lesion taking all deep and wide margins, and always needs stitches to close the wound. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old farmer has coarse deep wrinkles, leathery texture with deep rhomboidal furrowing of the posterior neck, mottled dyspigmentation, and several actinic keratoses. His sun-protected skin looks markedly younger. What distinguishes this from intrinsic chronological aging?","choices":["Intrinsic aging produces fine lines and dryness without hyperpigmentation or actinic keratoses, and affects sun-protected skin equally","Intrinsic aging spares the posterior neck while photoaging spares the face","Intrinsic aging is driven by ultraviolet A penetration into the dermis","Intrinsic aging produces coarse deep wrinkles and mottled dyspigmentation, while photoaging produces only fine lines and dryness","Only intrinsic aging responds to topical tretinoin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intrinsic chronological aging produces fine lines, dry skin, and decreased elasticity without hyperpigmentation or actinic keratoses, and it affects sun-protected skin equally. The disparity between exposed and protected skin is what identifies photoaging. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has a warm, tender, erythematous area on the right lower leg with indistinct borders and surrounding oedema. What feature most reliably distinguishes cellulitis from erysipelas?","choices":["Cellulitis is bilateral in most patients, whereas erysipelas is almost always confined to a single limb or site","Only erysipelas is caused by Streptococcus pyogenes","Cellulitis has indistinct borders that are not raised, whereas erysipelas is raised with a clear line of demarcation","Only cellulitis produces fever","Only cellulitis follows a portal of entry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erysipelas involves the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, producing a plaque raised above the surrounding skin with a clear line of demarcation. Cellulitis involves the deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue and therefore has borders that are indistinct and not raised. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has newly diagnosed mycosis fungoides confined to patches and plaques on the trunk, and his family asks why he has not been offered chemotherapy. What is the treatment philosophy in mycosis fungoides?","choices":["Surgical excision of all involved skin","No treatment at any stage, since the disease is indolent","Stage-directed, skin-directed therapy, because early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure disease or prevent progression and may cause premature death","Immediate combination systemic chemotherapy for all patients at diagnosis, because early aggressive treatment has been shown to cure disease and prevent progression","High-dose systemic corticosteroids indefinitely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management is complex. Early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure disease or prevent progression, and overly aggressive therapy may cause complications and premature death, so treatment is stage-directed and skin-directed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A clinician seeing a 38-year-old woman with chest pain lists the diagnoses her initial cues suggest, then gathers further data to decide which are consistent with what has been observed. What does the hypothetico-deductive method involve?","choices":["Applying an if-then rule from a published guideline","Treating signs and symptoms before a definitive diagnosis is reached","Proposing hypotheses and testing whether they are acceptable by deciding if the data are consistent with them","Ranking diagnoses purely by how common they are in the population","Recognising disease patterns automatically and quickly, without a deliberate or controlled analytical process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The hypothetico-deductive method proposes hypotheses and tests whether they are acceptable or not by deciding whether the data are consistent with what has been observed. It runs from initial history, examination, and screening cues through hypothesis generation to hypothesis evaluation. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man in the American Southeast has a painful lesion on the thigh with a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin and then a large area of erythema. What is this sign, and what organism is responsible?","choices":["Hutchinson sign, from a hobo spider bite","Crowe's sign, from a caterpillar reaction","The red, white, and blue sign, from a black widow spider bite","The delta-wing jet sign, from a mite infestation","The red, white, and blue sign, from a brown recluse spider bite"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The hallmark of a brown recluse bite is the red, white, and blue sign — a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin surrounded by a large erythematous area. Loxosceles reclusa has a dark brown violin-shaped marking on the cephalothorax and is abundant in the American Midwest and Southeast. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"An 84-year-old nursing home resident has thick flaking scale over the hands and trunk but reports no itching at all. What is this presentation, and what is the infection control implication?","choices":["Post-scabietic dermatitis, which is not infectious","Xerosis of aging, requiring only emollients","Psoriasis, requiring topical corticosteroids","Crusted scabies, in which the scale contains millions of mites and the patient is highly infectious despite the absence of pruritus","Crusted scabies, which is less infectious than ordinary scabies because the mites are trapped within the thick scale and cannot spread"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkeratotic or crusted scabies presents as thick flaking scale containing millions of mites. These patients are highly infectious, and pruritus is usually absent — which is exactly why the diagnosis is missed and outbreaks occur. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has scaly annular plaques of the groin and scaling between the toes, and a student asks how these infections are named. How are dermatophyte infections classified, and what does the naming convey?","choices":["By body location of the fungus, giving tinea capitis, barbae, corporis, manuum, cruris, and pedis","By the causative species, giving Trichophyton, Microsporum, and Epidermophyton infection","By the depth of invasion into the dermis","By the patient's age at presentation","By whether the infection is contagious"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophyte infections are classified by body location: tinea capitis for scalp, barbae for beard, corporis for body, manuum for hand, cruris for the inguinal creases, and pedis for feet. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman presents with the sudden appearance of two smooth, round bald patches on her scalp, each about 3 cm across, that developed over three weeks. The underlying scalp is smooth without scaling, erythema, or scarring. Short hairs that are narrow at the root and normal in calibre at the tip are visible at the margins. She has a history of eczema and her mother has hypothyroidism. What finding is pathognomonic for the diagnosis?","choices":["The sudden onset over three weeks","The round shape of the areas of hair loss","Exclamation point hairs at the margins of the patches","The personal history of atopy and family history of autoimmune disease","The absence of scarring at the affected sites"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exclamation point hairs are pathognomonic for alopecia areata. Their growth ability is changing in real time, which produces a hair that is thinner at the root but normal in calibre at the top — exactly the description given here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman presents with a change in one fingernail and arrives wearing dark polish on every nail. What examination step is specified when evaluating a nail for a possible neoplasm?","choices":["Obtain radiographs of all digits routinely","Remove polish and inspect every nail","Inspect only the symptomatic nail to avoid unnecessary examination","Defer examination until pigment has been present for a year","Perform a potassium hydroxide preparation before any inspection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. History and examination should document onset, evolution, trauma, bleeding, pain or cold sensitivity, prior treatments, medications, immune status, human papillomavirus risk, and personal or family melanoma history, and polish must be removed so that every nail can be inspected. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man whose brother died of melanoma asks how to monitor his own skin between annual clinic visits. What should patients be taught about melanoma self-examination?","choices":["Examination should focus on counting the total number of naevi","Only lesions larger than 6 mm need to be reported","Annual self-examination limited to sun-exposed skin","Self-examination is unnecessary provided an annual whole-body clinician examination including the scalp and nails occurs","Monthly self-examination using the ABCDE and ugly-duckling principles, including scalp, back, palms, soles, and nails"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Patients should perform monthly self-examination using ABCDE and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles, and nails, with prompt assessment of evolution, bleeding, or ulceration. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has multiple tense, clear fluid-filled lesions on the trunk, the largest measuring 1.8 cm. What is the correct term for the largest lesion?","choices":["Vesicle","Wheal","Pustule","Bulla","Nodule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A bulla is a fluid-filled lesion greater than 1 cm. At 1.8 cm this lesion is above the threshold that separates it from a vesicle. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man is admitted with extensive flaccid bullae and painful erosions involving the mouth, chest, and groin. Biopsy demonstrates acantholysis and immunofluorescence confirms pathogenic antibodies. What is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Oral doxycycline with nicotinamide","Urgent treatment with rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone","Dapsone as single-agent therapy","Low-dose methotrexate with folic acid supplementation","Ultrapotent topical corticosteroids alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus is a life-threatening blistering disorder requiring urgent treatment, initially rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone. When steroids are used they are usually paired with a steroid-sparing agent such as azathioprine or mycophenolate so the patient can eventually be transitioned off. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 7-year-old girl is being treated for tinea capitis and her school has asked whether she must stay at home. What advice about school attendance and household measures is correct?","choices":["Treatment can be stopped as soon as itching and scaling improve","The child must remain out of school until the scalp is completely clear","School exclusion is generally unnecessary once effective treatment has begun, and personal hair items should not be shared","Household contacts should all take oral antifungal therapy","No household measures are needed because transmission is only from person to person by direct contact with an infected individual"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. School exclusion is generally unnecessary once effective treatment has started. Patients should avoid sharing personal hair items and should clean clippers, combs, brushes, bedding, and hats, and household contacts may use a sporicidal shampoo. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has dermatomal pain but no vesicular eruption. What is this presentation called?","choices":["Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Postherpetic neuralgia","Disseminated zoster","The pre-eruptive phase, which will always be followed by lesions within 72 hours","Zoster sine herpete"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Some patients have pain without a vesicular eruption, which is termed zoster sine herpete. Others have vesicular eruptions without pain, although almost all patients have pain during the acute eruptive phase. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with diabetes presents with an extremely painful lesion on the back of the neck. Examination shows a deep, indurated plaque with several separate openings draining pus. He has malaise, chills, and a fever of 38.4 degrees Celsius. What is the lesion, and what is the mainstay of therapy?","choices":["Hidradenitis suppurativa, whose mainstay of therapy is topical clindamycin","A furuncle, whose mainstay of therapy is incision and drainage","An abscess, whose mainstay of therapy is warm soaks","A carbuncle, whose mainstay of therapy is incision and drainage","A carbuncle, whose mainstay of therapy is oral antibiotics alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A carbuncle is two or more confluent furuncles with separate heads, arising in several hair follicles, and systemic symptoms are more common with a carbuncle than a furuncle. Incision and drainage is the mainstay, supported by oral antibiotics. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has a flat pink-purple patch on one side of the face that has been present since birth and has grown in proportion with him. Which statement best describes a nevus flammeus?","choices":["Present at birth and involutes completely by school age","Present at birth, grows with the child, does not involute, and becomes darker and thicker over time","Appears about six months after birth, with a quick proliferative phase followed by involution over years","An acquired lesion formed by capillary proliferation in adults","A soft yellow plaque of lipid-laden macrophages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nevus flammeus is a congenital vascular lesion of dilated superficial dermal capillaries through the entire depth of the dermis, without endothelial proliferation. It is present at birth, grows in size with the child, does not involute, and becomes darker and thicker. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man develops an extremely pruritic papulovesicular eruption on the fingers ten days after being diagnosed with tinea pedis. A potassium hydroxide preparation from the finger lesions is negative, while one from the toe webs is positive. What three criteria establish this diagnosis?","choices":["A positive bacterial culture, absence of fungal elements, and response to antibiotics","Symmetric distribution, absence of pruritus, and spontaneous resolution without treatment","A dermatophyte infection elsewhere, fungal elements present at the eruption site, and resolution after treatment","A dermatophyte infection elsewhere on the body, absence of fungal elements at the eruption site, and resolution after the primary infection is treated","A positive Wood's lamp at the primary site, a negative potassium hydroxide preparation at the distant site, and histological confirmation on biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those are the three criteria for establishing an id reaction. The primary site is potassium hydroxide positive and the distant id site is potassium hydroxide negative, and the eruption occurs 1 to 2 weeks after the primary infection. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl had impetigo three weeks ago. She now presents with facial oedema, tea-coloured urine, and hypertension. Her mother asks whether the antibiotics failed. What is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["This is rheumatic fever following the skin infection","This is acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, and antibiotics do not prevent it because the immune response usually precedes treatment","This represents treatment failure requiring a change of antibiotic","This is an allergic reaction to the antibiotic used for the impetigo","This is acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, which would have been prevented had antibiotics been started earlier in the course of the skin infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis may follow impetigo, especially in 3 to 7 year olds, and is characterised by sudden oedema, haematuria, and hypertension. Antibiotics do not prevent it because activation of the immune response most often precedes antibiotic treatment. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man is found to have hundreds of small papules over the trunk and extremities consistent with generalised granuloma annulare. What screening is most appropriate?","choices":["Colonoscopy to screen for gastrointestinal malignancy","No screening is indicated for any variant of the condition","Fasting glucose and haemoglobin A1c to screen for diabetes","Chest radiograph to screen for bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy","Immunoglobulin A tissue transglutaminase antibodies to screen for celiac disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fasting glucose and haemoglobin A1c are used to screen for diabetes in generalised granuloma annulare, alongside a lipid panel and thyroid studies for associated systemic disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman is prescribed topical tretinoin for acne. What counselling is most important before she starts?","choices":["Expect increased tolerance to sun exposure while using the medication","Begin gradually, expect irritation and dryness, use sun protection, and observe pregnancy precautions","Apply the medication together with a high-potency topical corticosteroid to prevent irritation","Begin at full frequency immediately, since gradual introduction delays benefit","No pregnancy precautions are needed because the medication is applied topically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The important clinical considerations for topical retinoids are irritation, dryness, and photosensitivity; therapy should begin gradually, and pregnancy precautions apply. Common agents include adapalene, tretinoin, tazarotene, and trifarotene. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman presents with painful red lumps on both shins that appeared ten days ago. Examination shows bilateral tender erythematous nodules 1 to 5 cm on the anterior tibial surfaces. None has ulcerated, and several are fading to a bruise-like colour. She had a sore throat three weeks ago. What is the key distinguishing feature of this condition?","choices":["The nodules followed a streptococcal infection","The nodules are located on the anterior shins","The nodules are tender","The nodules do not ulcerate","The nodules are bilateral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That the nodules do not ulcerate is named as the key distinguishing feature. Erythema nodosum is a septal panniculitis without vasculitis, evolving over 3 to 6 weeks from bright red through the bruise-like colours the stem describes. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity has multiple soft growths in both axillae and on the neck. How is an acrochordon best described morphologically, and where do they characteristically occur?","choices":["A translucent papule over the distal interphalangeal joint","A soft pedunculated skin-coloured papilloma with a narrow stalk and broad tip, occurring in areas of friction such as the neck, axilla, and groin","A hard conical keratotic projection on sun-exposed skin","A bright red friable pedunculated papule that bleeds easily","A firm sessile nodule with a central hyperkeratotic keratin core occurring on weight-bearing surfaces such as the sole and the ball of the foot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An acrochordon is a fibroepithelial pedunculated papilloma with a narrow stalk and broad tip, occurring in areas of friction — the neck, axilla, and groin. Incidence is increased in women and in obesity, and they vary from over 1 mm to as large as 10 mm. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man is stung by a wasp and within minutes develops widespread urticaria, wheezing, and hypotension. What is the immediate treatment?","choices":["Epinephrine given intramuscularly or subcutaneously, with emergency transfer and supportive measures","A topical corticosteroid applied to the sting site","An oral antihistamine and observation at home","Cleaning the site, applying ice, and possibly injecting a local anaesthetic for pain control at the sting","Extensive local wound debridement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For an anaphylactic reaction to a hymenoptera sting the treatment is subcutaneous or intramuscular epinephrine with emergency department transfer and supportive measures. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has hypopigmented scaly macules over the upper back and shoulders confirmed as pityriasis versicolor. What is first-line treatment, and when is systemic therapy appropriate?","choices":["Topical therapy first line, with oral fluconazole or itraconazole reserved for extensive, recurrent, or refractory disease","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","Oral fluconazole for all patients at diagnosis, after reviewing hepatic risk, pregnancy status, and drug interactions","Topical nystatin as first-line therapy","Oral terbinafine as first-line therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is first line, including ketoconazole shampoo or cream, selenium sulfide, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox, or topical terbinafine. Systemic therapy with oral fluconazole or itraconazole is reserved for extensive, recurrent, or topical-refractory disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl presents with comedones, papules, and pustules of the face and upper back. What are the four factors involved in the pathogenesis of acne vulgaris?","choices":["Autoimmune follicular inflammation, androgen excess, and impaired barrier function","Follicular hyperkeratinisation, increased sebum production, Cutibacterium acnes proliferation, and inflammation","Apocrine gland occlusion, bacterial proliferation, sinus tract formation, and scarring","Follicular hyperkeratinisation, decreased sebum production, bacterial proliferation, and inflammation","Neurovascular dysregulation, innate immune dysfunction, Demodex overgrowth, and microbiome alteration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acne involves follicular hyperkeratinisation with excess keratin in the follicle, increased sebum production providing a growth medium, proliferation of Cutibacterium acnes, and inflammation. The temporal sequence among them is not fully understood. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with obesity has a well-demarcated erythematous patch in the inframammary fold with several small papules and pustules on adjacent skin just beyond the main area of redness. What finding identifies the organism, and what topical choice covers both possibilities?","choices":["Satellite lesions identify Candida, and a topical azole covers both Candida and dermatophytes","Coral-red fluorescence identifies Candida, and topical erythromycin is used","Honey-coloured crusting identifies Candida, and topical mupirocin is used","Central clearing identifies Candida, and topical terbinafine covers both organisms","Satellite lesions identify Candida, and topical nystatin covers both Candida and dermatophytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Satellite papules and pustules accompany the well-demarcated erythematous patches of candidal intertrigo. Topical nystatin treats Candida only, whereas topical azoles treat both Candida and dermatophytes. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 46-year-old runner has a hyperkeratotic lesion on the sole, and a student asks how to tell a callus from a wart at the bedside. How do the skin lines behave in a callus compared with a verruca vulgaris, and why does this matter?","choices":["Skin lines run through both, so the sign is not useful","Skin lines cannot be assessed on plantar skin","Skin lines are interrupted by a callus but run through a wart","Skin lines run through a callus but are interrupted by a wart, which distinguishes the two on inspection","Skin lines are interrupted by both lesions, so the sign does not distinguish between them on inspection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Skin lines run through both corns and calluses, whereas verruca vulgaris interrupts the skin lines. This is a bedside sign that requires no equipment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a probable but unconfirmed diagnosis, and a treatment with meaningful adverse effects is being considered. What factors govern the decision to treat when the diagnosis carries some uncertainty?","choices":["Treatment should always be given whenever any possibility of disease exists, without weighing the likelihood of success or the patient's ability to tolerate it","The probability of disease, the likelihood of treatment success, the patient's ability to tolerate treatment, and the balance of risk against benefit","Treatment should always be withheld until the diagnosis is certain","Only the probability of disease, independent of the treatment's risks","Only the cost of the treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The value of treatment versus no treatment is a linear function of the probability of disease, together with the likelihood of success and the ability to tolerate treatment, weighed as risk against benefit. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman in her third trimester develops erythematous patches whose margins are studded with small pustules, accompanied by fever, chills, malaise, diarrhea, nausea, and joint pains. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Generalised candidiasis","Impetigo herpetiformis","Pemphigoid gestationis","Guttate psoriasis","Impetigo"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Impetigo herpetiformis is the psoriasis of pregnancy, occurring typically in the third trimester with erythematous patches whose margins are studded with subcorneal pustules. Onset is accompanied by fever, chills, malaise, diarrhea, nausea, and arthralgia. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman and her 31-year-old husband both have plaque psoriasis and ask about the likelihood their future children will be affected. What is the most accurate counselling?","choices":["Psoriasis is not heritable, so their children carry no increased risk","Psoriasis is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, so about 50 percent of children are affected","When both parents have psoriasis, about 8 percent of their children develop the condition","When both parents have psoriasis, about 41 percent of their children develop the condition","Risk is conferred entirely by human leukocyte antigen B27, which can be tested to give an exact figure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The figures given are 8 percent when one parent has psoriasis and 41 percent when both do, reflecting a genetic predisposition in which PSORS1 is the major susceptibility locus. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 155"},{"q":"A 41-year-old janitorial supervisor reports several weeks of progressively dry, sore hands. She washes her hands frequently and handles detergents and cleaning solutions throughout her shift. Examination shows a glazed, well-demarcated erythematous eruption with scaling over the dorsal hands and distal forearms, ending abruptly at the wrists. She denies any new jewellery, gloves, cosmetics, or topical products, and patch testing performed previously was negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cutaneous candidiasis","Irritant contact dermatitis","Allergic contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis of the hands","Dyshidrotic eczema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Irritant contact dermatitis is the most common form of contact dermatitis and results from chemicals or friction that disrupt the skin barrier. It is frequently occupational, with healthcare and janitorial workers cited as the classic examples, and mild irritants such as soaps produce subacute symptoms progressing over weeks. Hands and forearms are the commonest sites. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has persistent acne, hirsutism, and irregular menses. What differential diagnosis should be considered?","choices":["Acne mechanica","Polycystic ovary syndrome, indicating androgen hypersecretion","Rosacea","Anabolic steroid use, causing a drug-induced acneiform eruption","Perioral dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Persistent acne in a hirsute woman with irregular or absent menses indicates androgen hypersecretion, and polycystic ovary syndrome is the named differential. Signs of hyperandrogenism should be sought during pre-treatment assessment. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a keratotic lesion on the ear that has enlarged over six weeks and now bleeds when he shaves. Which findings in a keratotic lesion are red flags for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Rapid growth, pain, bleeding, and ulceration","Colour variegation with border irregularity","A pearly translucent surface with telangiectasias","A stuck-on appearance with comedone-like openings","Sandpaper texture and small size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Red flags for squamous cell carcinoma include rapid growth, pain, bleeding, and ulceration, and the same features in an actinic keratosis prompt biopsy to exclude early carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
